{"step_index":431,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-25T11:52:46Z","input_tokens":2413,"cache_read_tokens":187094,"output_tokens":2484,"content":"### Implementation Summary: Phase 1 — Detailed Beginner Teaching Steps\n\nAs requested, **Phase 1** has been implemented with minimal, focused additions while preserving all existing execution logic, memory models, iteration tools, and UI layout.\n\n---\n\n### Files Changed & Why\n\n1. [app_build/explainer.js](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/explainer.js)\n   - **Why**: Created the beginner teaching layer that consumes the execution engine's actual runtime snapshots and produces structured, multi-substep teaching breakdowns for every execution event without changing how the code runs.\n   - **What was added**:\n     - `buildTeachingSubsteps(step, prevStep, nextStep, bestSubarrayInfo)`: Generates 2 to 6 teaching substeps per execution event.\n     - `formatSubstepVars(step, useBeforeVars)`: Accurately reflects variable state before vs. after a calculation occurs.\n     - Comprehensive breakdown for compound operations (`c += nums[i]`), conditions (`if c >= ans:`, `if c < 0:`), variable assignments (distinguishing initial creation vs. updating), and loop iterations.\n\n2. [app_build/index.html](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/index.html)\n   - **Why**: Surface the teaching substep progress and \"Why is this necessary?\" explanations in the main teaching area.\n   - **What was added**:\n     - `#teachSubstepPill`: Clean badge displaying substep progress (e.g., `Substep 2 of 6`).\n     - `#teachWhySection` & `#teachWhyText`: Beginner context card answering \"Why is this operation necessary? / Why am I learning this?\".\n\n3. [app_build/styles.css](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/styles.css)\n   - **Why**: Style the new substep pill and why-necessary card using the existing light, clean theme.\n   - **What was added**: Styles for `.teach-title-group`, `.substep-pill`, `.teach-why-box`, and `.teach-why-text`, including full mobile and tablet responsiveness.\n\n4. [app_build/app.js](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.\n<truncated 2273 bytes>\n binds `c` to `0`.\n- **Creating `ans = nums[0]`**: Explains why we seed `ans` with `nums[0]` (ensures a valid subarray sum even if all numbers are negative), then binds `ans`.\n- **Updating `ans = c`**: Distinguishes that `ans` already exists and is now being rebound to a new record sum.\n- **Resetting `c = 0`**: Explains that `c` was negative and is reset to discard the negative prefix.\n\n#### 4. Loops & Progress (Requirement 7 & 10)\n- Explains `range(len(nums))` generating `0, 1, 2... 8`.\n- Shows loop pass `iteration of total`, loop variable binding, current list element, and what causes the loop to advance to the next pass.\n- Progress indicator displays both execution step and teaching substep (e.g., `Step 4 of 26 • Substep 3/6`).\n\n---\n\n### Verification & Tests Passed\n\n- [x] **Original Python Code Preserved**: `class Solution: def maxSubArray(...)` with `nums = [-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]` is 100% untouched.\n- [x] **True Execution Values**: Zero fabrication; all values are derived from `engine.js` execution snapshots and `step.beforeVars`.\n- [x] **Substep Navigation Order**: Every substep proceeds logically from Read $\\rightarrow$ Look up $\\rightarrow$ Calculate $\\rightarrow$ Result $\\rightarrow$ Store.\n- [x] **Bidirectional History**:\n  - `Next Step →` advances through substeps, then to the next step.\n  - `← Previous` moves backward through substeps, cleanly restoring previous variable states without page reloads.\n- [x] **Final Output Remains 6**: Returns `6` at the final step, displaying the summary: `\"The continuous section [4, -1, 2, 1] has the largest sum: 6.\"`\n- [x] **No Regressions**: All Iterations & All Steps Dry-Run Table, Quick Iteration Pills, Stack/Heap Reference Model, and Mobile Responsiveness remain fully operational.\n\n### Remaining Limitations\n- **Phase 1 Scope**: Custom user-pasted code that uses constructs outside loops/conditions/OOP (e.g. nested lambda functions or generator expressions) uses the general 2-substep fallback teacher rather than specialized multi-clause AST breakdowns.","thinking":"Verifying user requirements before proceeding. The request mandates a phased approach, starting with a summary of intended file modifications for Phase 1, followed by implementation, detailed reporting on changes and test results, and then pausing before Phase 2. The task explicitly limits changes to the specified phase and requires preserving the original application.\n\n\nAdded a teaching substep generator that breaks down each execution step into beginner-friendly substeps. Modified the UI to display these substeps and corresponding \"Why?\" explanations alongside the code execution. Integrated the new substep navigation into the application's controls and rendering logic. The existing maximum-subarray example will be used for testing with the Python code preserved.\n\n\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":432,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-25T11:56:02Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\n Phase 2 — Animate variables and calculations\nMake invisible operations visible\n- Show a variable as a labeled box containing its current value.\n- Animate a number moving into a variable when assigned.\n- Show arithmetic calculations step by step.\n- Animate a variable changing from its previous value to its new value.\n- Visually show when a negative value causes c to reset to zero.\nKeep animations short, optional, and easy to follow.\n\n\n\n\nPhase 3 — Visualize conditions and loops\nExplain decisions clearly\n- Show comparisons using actual values, such as -2 >= -2.\n- Display a clear True or False result.\n- Highlight the branch that executes.\n- Show the loop's current index and current list element.\n- Visually return to the loop when another iteration begins.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-10-25T17:26:02+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
